  "answer": "The cleansing action of soap is due to its unique molecular structure. Soap molecules have two ends: one is hydrophilic (attracted to water) and the other is hydrophobic (attracted to grease and oil). When soap is added to water, the hydrophobic ends of the soap molecules attach to the grease and dirt on the surface of the cloth. The hydrophilic ends remain in contact with the water. With agitation, the soap molecules form clusters called micelles, which surround the dirt particles. These micelles remain suspended in water and can be rinsed away, thus cleaning the cloth.",
